# arvo. agent instructions

## What arvo is

arvo is a numeric foundation: exact-width primitives whose behaviour is
chosen by the consumer rather than by the container the machine happens
to offer. Everything is `#![no_std]`, no `alloc`, no platform
dependency, and every size is const at type level.

## The tree is empty right now, and that is deliberate

`mock/crates/` holds nothing and `mock/Cargo.toml` is an empty
workspace. This is not a broken checkout and it is not work in
progress. The previous crate layout was removed on purpose, because
arvo's canon is being written and the mutation order requires the
lower tiers to be cleared before the tier above them can change.

**So there is no current architecture to read, and the one git
remembers is a dead tier.** Do not reconstruct it, do not reason from
it, and do not treat a name that appears in git history as a name the
design still uses. Everything about the decomposition is open: how many
crates there are, what they are called, which layer owns what, and
whether the layering is the right shape at all.

An agent that goes looking for the old tree and builds on what it finds
is reattaching the tier that had to be detached for the current work to
be permitted. That has already happened twice.

## Where the authority actually is

The canon is being written at
`mock/research/202608072330_the-numeral-canon-panel/`. Two files there
carry what binds:

- `INTENTS.md` is op's stated intents, quoted with provenance. Op is
  the lead designer and the only ratifying human. This is the material
  that graduates into the canon.
- `RULES.md` is how that panel works, including the provenance ladder.

Everything else in that directory is agent output produced without a
recorded human decision. Presume it wrong where it conflicts with op,
and treat agreement between two unratified files as shared drift rather
than corroboration.

## Long-standing constraints, and their provenance, which is not what it looks like

These have governed arvo since before the canon work and are operative discipline now:

- `#![no_std]` everywhere. No `alloc`, no `Vec`, no `String`, no `Box`.
- Sizes are const. No runtime growth: no `.push(`, `.resize(`, `.extend(`.
- Monomorphisation is the dispatch. No `dyn`, no `TypeId`, no `std::any`.
- No platform dependency: no `std::thread`, `std::time`, `std::fs`, `std::net`.
- Public API positions use the stack's own primitives rather than bare
  `u8`..`u128` / `i8`..`i128` / `f32` / `f64` / `bool` / `usize`.

**None of them appears in `INTENTS.md`**, which holds op's ratified intents I1 through I12. They are
asserted in agent-authored places: the workspace rules, and this file's own predecessor. An earlier
version of this section called them op's intents. That was wrong, and it was written during the very
edit that was fixing this file for the same class of error.

Follow them. They are long-standing and nothing has overturned them. But do not cite them as ratified,
do not build a canon sentence on them without saying where they actually come from, and treat any
argument that depends on one of them as resting on unratified ground until op says otherwise.

This is load-bearing right now rather than pedantic: the erasure half of op's acceptance criterion is
argued, across several panel files, from the absence of `dyn` and `TypeId`. That argument is only as
settled as the constraint under it.

## What is open, including things that used to read as settled

The strategy axis exists: a numeric type's behaviour is selected by the
consumer, and the substrate provides the choice rather than making it.
That much is intent.

**The strategy set is not closed at four.** `Hot`, `Warm`, `Cold` and
`Precise` are what the previous design happened to carry, and op has
stated explicitly that the set is open to discussion and exploration.
Nothing may be written that presumes exactly those four, or that
presumes their meanings are fixed.

What each strategy weighs is likewise intent rather than rule. They are
all decided by measurement; what differs is which measurements each one
weighs most. Read `INTENTS.md` for op's own statement of each, and do
not compress those statements into a table of absolutes.

## Consumers

arvo is a library; real code lives downstream. hilavitkutin (the
pipeline execution engine) and vehje (the Clause language toolchain)
are the known consumers, and they drive which algorithm surfaces
eventually exist. arvo's API is not shaped by any one consumer; arvo
stays usable by anyone.

Which specific surfaces those consumers use is part of what the canon
work is redeciding, so no list of them is given here.

## Commit style

Format: `type: lowercase imperative message`. See
`skills/commit-style/SKILL.md`.

Byline policy is configured in `mock/agent/config.toml`
`[attribution]`. The built-in `check-byline.sh` hook enforces it at
commit time.

## Where things live

- `docs/DESIGN.md` is the generated canonical architecture.
- `docs/STRUCTURE.md` + graph are generated, covering crate and item index.
- `mock/DESIGN.md.tmpl`, `mock/PRINCIPLES.md.tmpl`,
  `mock/WORKFLOW.md.tmpl` are the authored sources.
- `mock/crates/*/DESIGN.md.tmpl` holds per-crate deep dives.
- `mock/design_rounds/` is the design round state machine. Do not rename
  files by hand; use `cargo mock lock` / `unlock` / `deprecate` /
  `close`.
- `mock/agent/` holds these instructions. Edit templates here, not the
  generated files under `.claude/` or `.github/`.

## What not to do

- Do not reason from the removed crate tree, or restore any part of it because a
  document mentions it. Several generated rule files still name it; they carry a
  banner saying so.
- Do not add `alloc`, `std`, `Vec`, `String`, `Box`, `dyn`, or `TypeId` anywhere,
  noting the provenance caveat above: these are long-standing and not in op's
  ratified intents.
- Do not add platform calls (`std::thread`, `std::time`, `std::fs`, `std::net`).
- Do not grow collections at runtime. All sizes are const.
- Do not edit files under `.claude/`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`,
  `.github/instructions/`, `.github/skills/`, or `.github/hooks/`. They
  regenerate from `mock/agent/` on every `cargo mock`.
- Do not hand-rename `design_rounds/*.md` suffixes. Use the subcommands.
